            <div xml:id="d240">

                <pb facs="0301" n="293" sameAs="frus:frus1941v03/pg293"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War 1939/15254: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Egypt</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Kirk</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Cairo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1941-09-18T17:00:00+02:00">September 18, 1941—5
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received September 20—8:40 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1422. My 1410, September 17, 10 p.m. I am confidentially informed that the
                    British Ambassador yesterday furnished the Egyptian Prime Minister at the
                    latter’s request a written statement to the effect that the declaration of Cairo
                    as an open city would be contrary to the successful prosecution of the war. The
                    Ambassador is said to have added that he trusted that the Prime Minister shared
                    this view and would so express himself in any further discussion of the matter
                    in such a way as to make it clear that he was expressing his own opinion rather
                    than merely communicating the British view.</p>
                <p>The Ambassador is also understood to have informed the Prime Minister that the
                    matter of bombing Rome in reprisal for the raid on <pb facs="0302" n="294"
                        xml:id="pg294"/> Cairo during the night of September 15–16 had been taken
                    under advisement, but that it was deemed inadvisable to take retaliatory action
                    at this time in the light of certain other considerations affecting the war
                    effort. The Prime Minister is said to have replied that under the circumstances
                    he would not press the matter further. It may be observed that news reports from
                    London appearing in today’s press here give the impression that the bombing of
                    Rome is contemplated.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Kirk</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
